<div style="width: 100%; padding: 20px; background-color: #f0f8ff; border: 2px solid #1a5276; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # What Is Renewable Energy? – Lesson # 1</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;"><i>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</i></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Grade:</b> 9th Grade (CEFR: A2.4)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Scenario:</b> Renewable Energy in Panama</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Theme:</b> What Is Renewable Energy?</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Analyze longer narratives. Interactive: Participate in group problem-solving sessions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b style="color: #1a5276;">Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can describe key ideas from longer narratives about renewable energy in Panama by summarizing main points and supporting details (e.g., "In the article about Panama's renewable energy projects, they explained how solar panels in rural areas provide electricity to homes, reduce reliance on fossil fuels, and improve daily life for families.")
Can participate in group problem-solving by sharing ideas and proposing realistic solutions for Panama's energy challenges, including rural areas (e.g., "We can install solar panels in remote villages to provide electricity for schools and reduce reliance on generators.")
Can actively participate by asking thoughtful questions to explore energy-saving ideas (e.g., "How does wind power help protect the environment?").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b><br>(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher begins by showing captivating pictures of various energy sources in Panama, such as solar panels on rooftops, wind turbines on hills, and hydroelectric dams. Students are asked: "What do you see in these pictures? What kind of power do you think these things generate? Where does the electricity in your home come from?" This activates prior knowledge and introduces the concept of energy sources.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models the pronunciation of key vocabulary words like "energy," "renewable," "solar," "wind," "preserve," and "sustainability," emphasizing stress in multisyllabic and compound words. The teacher also models simple sentences using gerunds ("<i>Saving energy</i> is very important for our planet.") and the present continuous ("The country <i>is investing</i> in green technology.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of new concepts and vocabulary. For example, for "renewable energy": "Does 'renewable energy' mean it finishes quickly or can we use it again and again?" (Expected: "Again and again.") "Is the sun a 'renewable source'?" (Expected: "Yes.") For "solar panels": "Are solar panels used to make electricity from the sun or from wind?" (Expected: "From the sun.")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b><br>(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Renewable Energy in Panama")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher presents a short, informative audio narrative (e.g., a simplified podcast excerpt or news report) titled "Panama's Green Power" (genre: informal narrative/report; register: informative; format: audio clip). The audio describes various renewable energy projects across Panama (solar, wind, hydropower) and their benefits for the environment and local communities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will listen to understand the main ideas and specific details about renewable energy sources and their benefits in Panama.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Listening (Gist):</b> Students listen for the main idea. Teacher asks: "What is the audio mainly about?" and "Which types of energy does Panama use?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Listening (True/False):</b> Students listen again and mark statements as True or False based on the audio. (e.g., "Panama is only using solar energy." "Renewable energy helps the environment.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Third Listening (Information Matching):</b> Students match key vocabulary words from the audio (e.g., 'solar panels', 'wind turbines', 'hydropower') with their descriptions or associated benefits mentioned in the recording (e.g., "provide electricity to rural areas," "generate clean energy," "preserve natural resources").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b><br>(Accuracy focus on Listening)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening for specific information, comprehension of vocabulary (energy, solar, wind, source, electricity, power, resource, generate, provide, panels, turbine, clean, renewable, sustainable), and grammatical structures (gerunds, present continuous, adverbial clauses for justification).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Listen and Complete):</b> The teacher plays specific sentences or short segments from the audio again. Students are given a worksheet with these sentences, but with key target vocabulary (e.g., 'generate', 'preserve', 'panels', 'renewable') or grammatical structures (e.g., a gerund or present continuous verb phrase) blanked out. Students listen carefully and fill in the missing words/phrases.
                        Example: "Panama ____ ____ (invest) in more ____ (clean) ____ (energy) projects." "____ (Save) energy helps ____ (preserve) our ____ (environment)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-Controlled Practice (Sentence Transformation with Listening):</b> The teacher reads aloud sentences about renewable energy. Students listen and then rewrite the sentences, replacing a highlighted word with a synonym or a phrase that was used in the original audio input, or changing a simple verb to a gerund form.
                        Example: Teacher: "Panama makes clean electricity using the sun." Students rewrite: "Panama is <i>generating</i> clean <i>power</i> using <i>solar energy</i>." Or, Teacher: "It is important to save resources." Students rewrite: "<i>Saving</i> resources is important <i>because</i> it helps <i>sustainability</i>."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction (Listen and Verify):</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher reads a series of statements, some true and some false, related to the audio and renewable energy. After each statement, partners discuss and agree on whether it is true or false, justifying their answer based on what they heard or learned, using target vocabulary and adverbial clauses.
                        Example: Teacher: "Wind turbines are easy to install everywhere." Student A: "False, because the audio mentioned they need windy areas." Student B: "Yes, <i>due to</i> the climate."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b><br>(Communicative task using language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Group Problem-Solving Discussion (listening-based simulation).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Description:</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). The teacher plays a new, short audio clip (1-2 minutes) describing a specific energy challenge faced by a remote rural community in Panama (e.g., high cost of electricity from generators, lack of consistent power, environmental pollution from fossil fuels). Students listen carefully to understand the problem.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">After listening, each group discusses and proposes at least two realistic solutions using renewable energy sources. They must justify their solutions using adverbial clauses and incorporate at least four new target vocabulary words (e.g., 'reliance', 'infrastructure', 'cost-effective'). They also practice using gerunds and the present continuous.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Example prompt after audio:</b> "You've just heard about the energy problem in El Copé. Discuss in your groups: What are the main challenges, and what renewable energy solutions can you propose for this community? Justify your ideas."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Output:</b> Groups verbally share their proposed solutions and justifications with the class. For instance: "We are proposing <i>installing</i> solar panels in El Copé <i>because</i> they are <i>cost-effective</i> and will <i>provide</i> clean <i>electricity</i>. <i>Reducing reliance</i> on old <i>generators</i> will <i>preserve</i> the <i>environment</i>."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">12-15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b><br>(Formative Assessment)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher Observation Checklist during group discussion and solution presentation. The teacher listens for overall comprehension of the audio problem, appropriate use of target vocabulary and grammar, and collaborative participation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Each group orally presents their two proposed solutions for the energy challenge, justifying their choices and demonstrating their listening comprehension of the problem described in the audio.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Listening Comprehension:</b> Clear understanding of the problem presented in the audio.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary Use:</b> Appropriate use of at least three target vocabulary words related to renewable energy (e.g., 'solar', 'wind', 'electricity', 'generate', 'sustainability').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar Application:</b> Correct use of gerunds and present continuous, and adverbial clauses for justification.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity and Coherence:</b> Solutions are clear, logical, and well-justified.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Active and respectful participation in group discussion.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> Intelligible pronunciation of key vocabulary (e.g., "renewable", "preservation").</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete the following prompts individually:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What was one new word or phrase about renewable energy that I learned today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What was one interesting idea I heard about how Panama is using renewable energy?</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What did you find easy or difficult about listening to and understanding today's topic on renewable energy?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. How can understanding renewable energy help you and Panama in the future?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson to engage students in listening and discussion about renewable energy? (e.g., visuals, audio quality, group dynamics).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for students who struggled with listening comprehension or vocabulary? (e.g., pre-teaching more specific vocabulary, providing a glossary, using simpler audio inputs, graphic organizers for note-taking).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully use gerunds, present continuous, and adverbial clauses to justify their solutions? If not, how can this be reinforced?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> "Today we listened to information about renewable energy in Panama and discussed solutions. Next class, we will *read* an article to learn even more details about these projects and their impact on our country's future."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">3-5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>